[
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-yokoyama-taikan-1",
    "title": "Autumn Leaves",
    "artist": "Yokoyama Taikan",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-yokoyama-taikan-1-94a2445d-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1931",
    "dimensions": "163.3×361.0 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1931 163.3×361.0 cm（each）\nWith its depiction of ripples of platinum‐tinged mud and deep red maple leaves on running water that has been colored ultramarine, this work shows the limpid nature of autumn.\nIt is the largest and greatest of Taikan’s works in the Adachi Museum collection, and it is brought out every year for the special autumn exhibition.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/yokoyama-taikan",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/4501_koyo.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-yokoyama-taikan-2",
    "title": "Selflessness",
    "artist": "Yokoyama Taikan",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-yokoyama-taikan-2-60500bfe-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1897",
    "dimensions": "75.4×46.7 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1897 75.4×46.7 cm\nKnown as the masterpiece that made a name for Taikan when he was 29 years old. “Selflessness” means achieving enlightenment in Zen Buddhism. Taikan represented enlightenment by drawing an innocent child.\nAn innovative idea that became the talk of Japanese society.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/yokoyama-taikan",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/4502_muga.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-yokoyama-taikan-3",
    "title": "Mountain after a Shower",
    "artist": "Yokoyama Taikan",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-yokoyama-taikan-3-3dd45625-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1940",
    "dimensions": "81.2×114.3 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1940 81.2×114.3 cm\nA scroll from Taikan’s Ten Scenes of Mt. Fuji and Ten Scenes of the Sea series painted in 1940, and said to be one of his best five India ink paintings. The mountain range depicted as a rain shower clears creates a flowing sensation; behind this, soaring in majesty, is Mt. Fuji.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/yokoyama-taikan",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/4503_ameharu.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-takeuchi_seiho-1",
    "title": "After a Shower",
    "artist": "Takeuchi Seiho",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-takeuchi_seiho-1-4d8b0501-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1928",
    "dimensions": "171.2×369.0 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1928 171.2×369.0 cm（each）\nSeiho succeeded in expressing the landscape after the rain, the branches and leaves waving in the wind, and the bird’s delicate movements, with his excellent skill in portrayal and composition.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/takeuchi_seiho",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/4601_usei.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-takeuchi_seiho-2",
    "title": "Fireside",
    "artist": "Takeuchi Seiho",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-takeuchi_seiho-2-a4f9acc6-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1935",
    "dimensions": "61.0×72.0 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1935 61.0×72.0 cm\nThe expressions on the two puppies, which appear to be filled with a pleasing warmth, invite the viewer to smile almost unconsciously.\nThe fireside is depicted solely through the treatment of the poker and ash fragments, yet it is fully realized down to the warmth it generates. It truly is a painting that could only be from the hand of Seiho.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/takeuchi_seiho",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/4602_robe.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-kawai-gyokudo-1",
    "title": "Moonlit Evening",
    "artist": "Kawai Gyokudo",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-kawai-gyokudo-1-504de4bb-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1913",
    "dimensions": "182.6×99.7 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1913 182.6×99.7 cm\nA fisherman and his children hurrying down the road home are bathed in the light of the moon on an autumn evening.\nThe painting shows a peaceful view of nature and can be said to bring together very nicely Gyokudo’s gentle disposition.\nIt is a work filled with pastoral mood.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/kawai-gyokudo",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/4701_yudukiyo.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-kawai-gyokudo-2",
    "title": "Spring Drizzle",
    "artist": "Kawai Gyokudo",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-kawai-gyokudo-2-1930f3bd-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1942",
    "dimensions": "44.5×56.8 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1942 44.5×56.8 cm\nIn the foggy spring rain, mountain cherry blossoms are depicted mistily behind kakei, a traditional Japanese bamboo water pipe. The trees and their young, fresh leaves are beautiful with a calm atmosphere, having gotten wet from the rain. A water wheel, grass and a farm lady walking on a small path give a sense of living with nature. This would be regarded as an honorable work that resonates in Japanese sensibility.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/kawai-gyokudo",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/4702_harusame.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-hishida_shunso-1",
    "title": "Cat and Plum Blossoms",
    "artist": "Hishida Shunso",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-hishida_shunso-1-d5f74cab-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1906",
    "dimensions": "118.0×49.8 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1906 118.0×49.8 cm\nHishida Shunso is noted for his depictions of cats, and his painting, “Black Cat”, is quite famous.\nThe work here was completed four years before that one, and one can see in the feline shown here that he had already achieved a fusion of realism and decorative art.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/hishida_shunso",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/4801_nekoume.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-hishida_shunso-2",
    "title": "Hydrangeas",
    "artist": "Hishida Shunso",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-hishida_shunso-2-97f6bc8e-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1902",
    "dimensions": "54.5×112.2 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1902 54.5×112.2 cm\nAlthough this work was produced during the period when Yokoyama Taikan along with his morotai method was not accepted, it is an exquisitely sophisticated work. Despite the colorfulness of the hydrangeas, the calculated composition and subdued tones evoke their innate feel of lonely melancholia.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/hishida_shunso",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/4802_ajisai.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-uemura_shoen-1",
    "title": "Daughter Miyuki",
    "artist": "Uemura Shoen",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-uemura_shoen-1-f2411bc1-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1914",
    "dimensions": "153.0×84.0 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1914 153.0×84.0 cm\nDepicts Akizuki Miyuki, the heroine of the Joruri drama, Sho‐utsushi Asagao‐banashi.\nSensing someone approaching while she gazes at a paper fan received from the one she adores, Miyuki glances behind her, hurriedly hiding the fan in her sleeve.\nThis masterpiece by Shoen Uemura from the Taisho period （1912‐1926） beautifully captures the heroine’s charming, innocent look.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/uemura_shoen",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/4901_musume-miyuki.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-uemura_shoen-2",
    "title": "Woman Waiting for the Moon to Rise",
    "artist": "Uemura Shoen",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-uemura_shoen-2-40c63b13-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1944",
    "dimensions": "73.0×86.0 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1944 73.0×86.0 cm\nThe depiction of this woman leaning on a railing has been executed with a simple composition, and what’s more this work makes almost miraculous use of the unpainted area and spacious depth that is a feature of Japanese painting. This work is a masterpiece of the mature period of Uemura Shoen, who idealized the dignity of the female form in her work.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/uemura_shoen",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/4902_taigetsu.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-kobayashi_kokei-1",
    "title": "Yang Guifei",
    "artist": "Kobayashi Kokei",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-kobayashi_kokei-1-066e07bc-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1951",
    "dimensions": "164.0×83.0 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1951 164.0×83.0 cm\nThis work is based on the Noh play performed by Noguchi Kanesuke of the Hosho School. The quiet tenshion is transmitted well by the slightly sway of the bead curtain and the highly restrained movements of the actor. It is a fine example of Kokei’s work in his mature period.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/kobayashi_kokei",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5001_yokohi.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-kobayashi_kokei-2",
    "title": "Magnolia Tree",
    "artist": "Kobayashi Kokei",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-kobayashi_kokei-2-0aebeda3-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1919",
    "dimensions": "140.0×50.0 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1919 140.0×50.0 cm\nThis work was painted during the transition years when he went from painting elegant historical paintings to harsh observations of nature and displays the painstaking care of his latter years. The work recalls to mind the sheer beauty of Japanese-style paints and pantings.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/kobayashi_kokei",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5002_mokuren.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-hashimoto_kansetsu-1",
    "title": "Dogs from Europe",
    "artist": "Hashimoto Kansetsu",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-hashimoto_kansetsu--67a4e0f6-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1941",
    "dimensions": "164.0×183.0 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1941 164.0×183.0 cm（each）\nOn the right is a black and white spotted Borzoi, and on the left are two greyhounds.\nThe painting’s name in Japanese, Token, refers to imported dogs. The lustrousness of the Borzoi’s fur is reflected in the gold tinged background, and it goes well with the deep red of the tree peonies.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/hashimoto_kansetsu",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5101_token-zu.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-hashimoto_kansetsu-2",
    "title": "Summer Evening",
    "artist": "Hashimoto Kansetsu",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-hashimoto_kansetsu--2fdf49c9-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1941",
    "dimensions": "108.1×127.5 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1941 108.1×127.5 cm\nA beautiful harmony of white is woven together in this work, between the whiteness of the moonflowers floating in the twilight and the equally ivory fox resting on the ground.\nIt also demonstrates another wonderful harmony, that between a realism learned from the Shijo School and the grace of Kansetsu. It evokes both a clear fragrance and cool dignity.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/hashimoto_kansetsu",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5102_kayu.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-yasuda_yukihiko-1",
    "title": "Wang Zhaojun",
    "artist": "Yasuda Yukihiko",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-yasuda_yukihiko-1-d254d190-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1947",
    "dimensions": "88.0×55.3 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1947 88.0×55.3 cm\nWang Zhaojun was a mistress of great beauty who served the Emperor of ancient China in the earlier Han period. A curious fate caused her to marry the leader of an enemy country. This work elegantly depicts her personality and her resolution to accept this fate.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/yasuda_yukihiko",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5201_oshokun.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-kawabata_ryushi-1",
    "title": "Passion",
    "artist": "Kawabata Ryushi",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-kawabata_ryushi-1-e115df00-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1934",
    "dimensions": "168.2×168.5 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1934 168.2×168.5 cm\nThe word aizen can refer to sexual passion or carnal desire, but here one sees the tender love of a couple. It shows an instant wherein a brace of mandarin ducks are visible amid the deep blue of the pond and red of the maple leaves.\nIt is an important work that harmonizes ornamentation with realism in a wondrous fashion.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/kawabata_ryushi",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5401_aizen.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-kawabata_ryushi-2",
    "title": "Floral Offering",
    "artist": "Kawabata Ryushi",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-kawabata_ryushi-2-72f29e62-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1940",
    "dimensions": "166.6×115.0 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1940 166.6×115.0 cm\nThe painting is an excellent work that is brilliantly and elegantly executed and does full justice to the title. Ryushi is a singularly unique artist in that he turned from western-style painting to Japanese-style painting after visiting America at the age of 28. It is truly characteristic of the artist to depict an arrangement of peonies in a bamboo basket on a large canvas.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/kawabata_ryushi",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5402_kenka.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-tsuchida_bakusen-1",
    "title": "Still Life – Salmon Slices and Sardines",
    "artist": "Tsuchida Bakusen",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-tsuchida_bakusen-1-0ecfaaaf-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1924",
    "dimensions": "46.5×52.0 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1924 46.5×52.0 cm\nThis work highlights the Japanese painting style of taking a blank space and subject, while the realism of Western painting can be also seen in the depiction of the raw fish and the dried fish, ensuring that they can be clearly distinguished. This work was produced soon after Bakusen returned from Europe, offering the remarkable influence of Western painting. The painter, however, skillfully integrated the essence of Western painting with that of Japanese painting.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/tsuchida_bakusen",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5501_seibutsu.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-tsuchida_bakusen-2",
    "title": "Sunset Hibiscus",
    "artist": "Tsuchida Bakusen",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-tsuchida_bakusen-2-d51d7b7d-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1932",
    "dimensions": "152.0×69.5 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1932 152.0×69.5 cm\nThe flowers favored by Bakusen are clear and fragrant, including the Sunset Hibiscus depicted here as well as mustard, peonies, morning glories, lotus, and chrysanthemum. This work is also soothing, and Bakusen’s superlative grace floats over it.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/tsuchida_bakusen",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5502_oshokki.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-sakakibara_shiho-1",
    "title": "Unripe Plum Blossoms",
    "artist": "Sakakibara Shiho",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-sakakibara_shiho-1-7910e3ed-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1918",
    "dimensions": "176.0×114.5 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1918 176.0×114.5 cm（each）\nThis work by Shiho was the first item presented to the National Painting Creation Society Kokuga Sosaku Kyokai, which was formed as a venue through which to present modern works of Japanese painting. The painting evokes both a vitality filled with hope for new challenges and a Romanticist yearning for the West.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/sakakibara_shiho",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5601_aoume.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-sakakibara_shiho-2",
    "title": "Japanese White‐eyes and Plum Blossoms",
    "artist": "Sakakibara Shiho",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-sakakibara_shiho-2-60c0c683-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1939",
    "dimensions": "45.2×50.3 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1939 45.2×50.3 cm\nHere, Japanese white eyes flitter about merrily as their wings fill with the still‐cool winds of early spring.\nIt can be said that the flower and bird work is that of a painter who feels the limitless pleasure of engaging in a dialogue with nature.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/sakakibara_shiho",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5602_baikagunkin.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-sakakibara_shiho-3",
    "title": "Persimmon and Bamboo",
    "artist": "Sakakibara Shiho",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-sakakibara_shiho-3-b1f63c9d-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1967",
    "dimensions": "53.0×23.5 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1967 53.0×23.5 cm\nThis persimmon, the only one remaining on the bare tree branch, is at one time a symbol of nature’s truth yet also seems to exist at the will of the universe. Going beyond a mere exchange with nature, this work evokes a view of Shiho in his later years, when he had achieved his own sense of unity with nature.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/sakakibara_shiho",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5603_kakitake.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-hayami_gyoshu-1",
    "title": "New Leaves",
    "artist": "Hayami Gyoshu",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-hayami_gyoshu-1-6f030c2e-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1915",
    "dimensions": "125.0×81.0 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1915 125.0×81.0 cm\nThis painting filled with new leaves budding from treetops of all types transmits well the feeling of the artist. The work is filled with a freshness that is almost unthinkable for something executed early in the Taisho period （1910‐1926）.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/hayami_gyoshu",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5701_shinryoku.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-ito_shinsui-1",
    "title": "Girls Full of Dreams",
    "artist": "Ito Shinsui",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-ito_shinsui-1-3e2ba3d0-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1952",
    "dimensions": "145.5×177.9 cm",
    "medium": "Japanese Painting",
    "category": "Modern Japanese Paintings",
    "description": "1952 145.5×177.9 cm\nThis is a commemorative work in which Shinsui established his own unique group portrait style.\nThe faces of the girls and their sailor uniforms each have a subtlety of expression and have been executed separately, while the totality produces a clear beauty.\nThe girl in the foreground with her hair in plaits is modeled upon Shinsui’s own daughter, Asaoka Yukiji, who became famous as an actress earlyon.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/ito_shinsui",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/5801_yume-ookikoro.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-kitaoji_rosanjin-1",
    "title": "Jar in Overglaze Enamel and Gold",
    "artist": "Kitaoji Rosanjin",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-kitaoji_rosanjin-1-316624a5-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "",
    "dimensions": "",
    "medium": "Ceramics",
    "category": "Ceramic Works",
    "description": "c.1940 34.6 cm tall\nAlso called gold brocaded porcelain, this style is named after what looks like gold brocade laid upon the object, an effect achieved through designs in gold leaf and powder on the object being fired. This is one of Rosanjin’s most dazzling and gorgeous works, and no similar vase exists.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/kitaoji_rosanjin",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/7301_kinrande-tsubo.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-hayashi_yoshio-1",
    "title": "Angel Taking a Nap",
    "artist": "Hayashi Yoshio",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-hayashi_yoshio-1-f8a180ec-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1996",
    "dimensions": "45.2×53.0 cm",
    "medium": "",
    "category": "Pictures for Children",
    "description": "1996 45.2×53.0 cm\nIn the warm spring sunshine, a small angel is sleeping comfortably, and a rabbit and birds are sleeping next to the angel. Their facial expressions are peaceful, implying the painter’s deep affection for children.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/hayashi_yoshio",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/7501_tenshino-ohirune.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-hirakushi_denchu-1",
    "title": "Bodhisattva Incarnate",
    "artist": "Hirakushi Denchu",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-hirakushi_denchu-1-f9079e04-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "1911",
    "dimensions": "",
    "medium": "Wood Carving",
    "category": "Wood Carvings",
    "description": "1911 68.0 cm tall\nThis is the figure of Yuima, a fictional character who is said to have answered questions from Monju Bosatsu, a Bodhisattva of wisdom, with only silence. The mouth is closed in a straight line, and his eyes are piercing.\nA furious spirit and a sense of tension are full throughout the body. This is a marvelous work that was created when his expression skills in Buddhist sculptures were at their peak.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/hirakushi_denchu",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/8001_yuima-ichimoku.jpg"
  },
  {
    "id": "adachi-col-yonehara_unkai-1",
    "title": "South Sea Kwan Yin",
    "artist": "Yonehara Unkai",
    "imageUrl": "https://pub-396fad1f96754c2f816f260faf970e63.r2.dev/artworks/adachi-collection/adachi-col-yonehara_unkai-1-6648aba3-imageUrl.webp",
    "year": "",
    "dimensions": "",
    "medium": "Wood Carving",
    "category": "Wood Carvings",
    "description": "133.0 cm\nGyoran Kannon, a Bodhisattva with a fish basket, is one of the 33 Kannons, and is originated from a tale in the Tang Dynasty that a beautiful woman who sold fish was in fact the incarnation of Kannon. The gentle, elegant figure carved with moderate lines is suggestive of Unkai’s great skill.",
    "source": "Adachi Museum of Art",
    "link": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/en/archives/collection/yonehara_unkai",
    "original_imageUrl": "https://www.adachi-museum.or.jp/admin/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/8101_nankaikannon-1.jpg"
  },
